WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.100
Speaker 1: There were some genuinely&nbsp;
concerning news items yesterday related&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:09.540
to the elections and one of them came out of&nbsp;
Ron DeSantis is Florida as well as Missouri,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:17.640
Washington Post reports. Florida, Missouri tell&nbsp;
Justice Department voting monitors to stay outside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:23.760
of polling places. Federal monitors would need&nbsp;
local permission to observe activity inside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:29.340
election sites. The administration of Governor Ron&nbsp;
DeSantis has refused to allow Justice Department&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:34.080
election monitors to go inside polling locations&nbsp;
in South Florida, saying in a letter it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:39.000
be counterproductive and potentially undermine&nbsp;
confidence in the election. Monday, the Justice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:45.360
Department had announced it would send monitors to&nbsp;
64 jurisdictions nationwide, up from 44 in 2020,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:51.540
to evaluate how are elections being conducted amid&nbsp;
a wave of threats to workers working at elections,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:56.760
politicians and allegations of voter intimidation.&nbsp;
Miami Dade, Broward and Palm Beach counties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:02.220
were slated to receive the Justice Department's&nbsp;
Civil Rights Division monitors. Florida federal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:07.020
monitors were sent to some Florida localities&nbsp;
in 2020, but they stayed outside because of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:11.520
pandemic. Brad McVay, the chief counsel for the&nbsp;
Florida Department of State, said in a letter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:17.640
those monitors will not be allowed inside polling&nbsp;
places under Florida law. McVay said the Florida&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:23.580
secretary of state's office, overseen by DeSantis,&nbsp;
would instead send its own monitors right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:31.140
DeSantis is on the ballot. It's totally logical&nbsp;
to send DeSantis his own monitors to make sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:36.060
everything is on the up and up. That makes&nbsp;
sense, right? That makes a lot of sense. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:43.200
there is a debate here as to whether DeSantis&nbsp;
has the authority to stop the monitoring. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:49.740
what The Washington Post article says is while the&nbsp;
Justice Department is permitted to place monitors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:54.900
inside polling locations, the department must&nbsp;
receive permission from local election officials.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:59.340
The Justice Department has said it&nbsp;
followed standard procedure on Tuesday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:05.100
has monitors outside polling locations in Florida&nbsp;
and Missouri, where state officials said they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:13.440
would not be welcomed inside. This is not some big&nbsp;
smoking gun. It's just another example of how the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:19.740
right is all about. We need monitors, we need&nbsp;
observers, we need this, that. The other thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:25.740
we will train you to become such an individual.&nbsp;
Oh, the DOJ wants to send. Oh, now that would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:31.560
counterproductive to have them inside. I thought&nbsp;
more monitoring is good and it's just another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:39.720
little data point about how their principles&nbsp;
are worthless. Monitoring elections is good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:45.540
if it's their monitors, if it's their&nbsp;
organizations, but if it's the DOJ,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:53.880
all of a sudden we don't need that. It would be&nbsp;
obtrusive. It would be counterproductive. Not a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:58.440
surprise that as soon as it's inconvenient,&nbsp;
they abandon their so-called principles.

